**Verdict: CHALLENGE (scoped)** — `docs/specs/fleet-opt-OPT3-OPT5-8321d9d-SEC-verdict.md` @`bba1d3b`, sent `sec→tl` (id `378869d1`, round 1):

- **ACCEPT** OPT-3 attribution (A5/A7) + OPT-5 hygiene (A8/A9/A10) — no injection surface, git via argv (no shell).
- **BLOCK the A6 silent-consumption re-queue leg.** Adversarial probe proved only 1 of 3 contract parts (§2.2) holds:
  - **D1** — re-emit wake **self-cancelled**: `_requeue_tickets` writes `wake__role__<env_id>.touch`, then `_service_wake._unlink_wakes` deletes that same path, and the name stays in `pending_wakes`' `seen` → never re-fires. A stuck ticket with no further traffic is never re-driven = the silent loss A6 exists to prevent.
  - **D2** — no `failed_no_commit` ledger flag (spec mandates it); only a stdout print.
  - **D3** — the green test asserts store `mark_unread` only, never re-delivery — it masks D1/D2.
- **Not a release stop:** internal tooling, no injection/auth/exposure. I explicitly verified the counterpart — the same mechanism that breaks re-delivery also **bounds the re-queue spawn-loop**, so there's no livelock/DoS.

Resolves path handed to TL (make re-emit real + ledger row + end-to-end test, **or** delete the dead `write_wake` and prove next-wake re-drives). Inbound marked read; inbox clear; OPT-1 WIP left untouched (committed only my doc).
